  "title": "How to Solve Quadratics by Factorising",
  "content": "<p>A <strong>quadratic equation</strong> has the form \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\). To solve by factorising:</p><p>1. Rearrange so one side equals zero</p><p>2. Factorise the quadratic expression</p><p>3. Set each bracket equal to zero and solve</p><p>This works because if \\(A \\times B = 0\\), then either \\(A = 0\\) or \\(B = 0\\).</p><p><strong>Common pitfall:</strong> Dividing both sides by \\(x\\) loses a solution (\\(x = 0\\)). Always factorise instead.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Solve</strong> \\(x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Already equals zero</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> \\((x - 2)(x - 3) = 0\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> \\(x - 2 = 0\\) or \\(x - 3 = 0\\)</p><p><strong>Step 4:</strong> \\(x = 2\\) or \\(x = 3\\)</p>"
 },
